---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base
  title="Compound interest calculator: what savings and investments grow to"
  description="Work out what a lump sum and monthly deposits grow to at a given interest rate, year by year, with the contributions and the interest kept separate."
>
  <h1>Compound interest calculator</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Money left alone earns interest on its interest. That second part, the interest on
    interest, is what this page counts. Put in a starting amount, a monthly deposit if you
    make one, a rate and a number of years, and you get the final figure with the growth shown
    year by year.
  </p>
    <figure class="fig fig--money"><img src="/img/money.webp" alt="Coins on a table with a young plant growing from them" width="1200" height="514" decoding="async"></figure>
    <nav class="onpage" aria-label="On this page"><p class="onpage-title">On this page</p><ul><li><a href="#the-formula">The formula</a></li><li><a href="#one-example-worked-through">One example, worked through</a></li><li><a href="#questions-people-ask">Questions people ask</a></li></ul></nav>

  <div class="sheet">
    <div class="field-grid">
      <div>
        <label for="principal">Starting amount</label>
        <input type="number" id="principal" inputmode="decimal" min="0" step="any" value="10000" />
      </div>
      <div>
        <label for="monthly">Added each month</label>
        <input type="number" id="monthly" inputmode="decimal" min="0" step="any" value="200" />
      </div>
      <div>
        <label for="rate">Yearly interest rate (%)</label>
        <input type="number" id="rate" inputmode="decimal" min="0" max="100" step="any" value="7" />
      </div>
      <div>
        <label for="years">Years</label>
        <input type="number" id="years" inputmode="numeric" min="1" max="60" step="1" value="10" />
      </div>
    </div>
    <button class="btn btn--primary" id="calc" type="button">Work out the growth</button>
    <div id="out" aria-live="polite" style="margin-top:1.5rem;"></div>
    <p class="result-note">
      This is arithmetic, not a forecast and not financial advice. Real investments move up
      and down, charge fees, and get taxed. Savings accounts pay what they pay, and the rate
      changes. Treat the answer as what the numbers would do if the rate held steady, which
      it will not.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="the-formula">The formula</h2>
  <p>
    For a lump sum: A = P × (1 + r/n)<sup>nt</sup>, where P is the starting amount, r the yearly
    rate as a decimal, n the number of times a year interest is added (12 for monthly), and t
    the years. Monthly deposits are the same shape summed up: each deposit compounds for the
    months it sits there, which works out to deposit × [((1 + r/n)<sup>nt</sup> − 1) ÷ (r/n)].
    This page compounds monthly, the way most savings accounts and investment projections do.
  </p>

  <h2 id="one-example-worked-through">One example, worked through</h2>
  <p>
    Start with 10,000 at 7% a year for 10 years, nothing added: 10,000 × (1 + 0.07/12)<sup>120</sup>
    = <strong>20,096.61</strong>. The money roughly doubles, and only half of that second
    10,000 was interest on the original sum in any simple sense. Add 200 a month on top and
    the deposits grow to 34,616.96 by themselves. Together: you put in 34,000 over the decade
    and end with <strong>54,713.58</strong>, of which 20,713.58 is interest. That gap, the
    interest being more than half of what you paid in, is the whole point of starting early.
  </p>

  <h2 id="questions-people-ask">Questions people ask</h2>
  <dl class="faq">
    <dt>What rate should I put in?</dt>
    <dd>
      For a savings account, the rate on the account. For long term stock market investing,
      people often test 5% to 8% a year before inflation, because that brackets the long run
      historical average in many markets, but it arrives in lumps, never as a steady line.
      Anyone who promises you a fixed high rate is selling something.
    </dd>
    <dt>Why does the interest in later years get so much bigger?</dt>
    <dd>
      Because the pot it is calculated on keeps growing. In year one, 7% of 10,000 is 700. By
      year ten of the example above the pot is near 50,000, so one year of growth is over
      3,000. The rate never changed. The base did.
    </dd>
    <dt>Does this include inflation or tax?</dt>
    <dd>
      No. To think of it in real terms, a rough method is to use a rate about 2 to 3 points
      lower, which stands in for inflation. Tax depends on the account and the country, and
      no calculator page can know yours.
    </dd>
  </dl>

  <p class="meta">Method: standard compound interest formula, monthly compounding, deposits at month end. Page last checked 13 November 2026.</p>

  <script>
    const $ = (id) => document.getElementById(id);
    const out = $('out');
    const money = (n) => n.toLocaleString('en-GB', { minimumFractionDigits: 2, maximumFractionDigits: 2 });

    function compute() {
      const P = parseFloat($('principal').value) || 0;
      const pmt = parseFloat($('monthly').value) || 0;
      const rate = (parseFloat($('rate').value) || 0) / 100;
      const years = Math.min(60, Math.max(1, parseInt($('years').value, 10) || 1));
      const r = rate / 12;
      let rows = '';
      for (let y = 1; y <= years; y++) {
        const n = y * 12;
        const lump = P * Math.pow(1 + r, n);
        const deps = r === 0 ? pmt * n : pmt * ((Math.pow(1 + r, n) - 1) / r);
        const total = lump + deps;
        const paid = P + pmt * n;
        rows += '<tr' + (y === years ? ' class="point"' : '') + '><td>Year ' + y + '</td><td class="num">' + money(paid) + '</td><td class="num">' + money(total - paid) + '</td><td class="num">' + money(total) + '</td></tr>';
      }
      const n = years * 12;
      const lump = P * Math.pow(1 + r, n);
      const deps = r === 0 ? pmt * n : pmt * ((Math.pow(1 + r, n) - 1) / r);
      const total = lump + deps;
      const paid = P + pmt * n;
      out.innerHTML =
        '<p class="result-figure">' + money(total) + '</p>' +
        '<p>You put in ' + money(paid) + '. The interest adds ' + money(total - paid) + '. Here is the pot at the end of each year:</p>' +
        '<table><thead><tr><th scope="col">Year</th><th scope="col" class="num">You put in</th><th scope="col" class="num">Interest so far</th><th scope="col" class="num">Pot</th></tr></thead><tbody>' + rows + '</tbody></table>';
    }

    $('calc').addEventListener('click', compute);
    compute();
  </script>
</Base>
